---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Pet Insurance Articles — WasPetInsuranceWorthIt.com"
  description="Data-driven research articles on pet insurance costs, exclusions, timing, breed risk, and the savings account alternative. No sales pitch."
>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">

    <header class="mb-10">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold mb-4 leading-tight" style="color:#1B6B6D;">
        Pet Insurance Research Articles
      </h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
        Data-forward analysis on pet insurance costs, exclusions, timing, and alternatives.
        No affiliate links. No insurance company sponsorship. All articles use the same
        neutral, consumer-research framing as the calculator.
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Cost & Pricing -->
    <section class="mb-10">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-wide mb-4">Costs &amp; Pricing</h2>
      <div class="space-y-4">

        <a href="/how-much-does-pet-insurance-cost/" class="block rounded-xl border border-gray-200 bg-white p-5 hover:border-gray-300 transition-colors group">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2 group-hover:underline" style="color:#1B6B6D;">
            How Much Does Pet Insurance Cost Per Month?
          </h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-3">
            Full premium reference by pet type, breed risk, deductible, and reimbursement level.
            Includes the industry loss ratio comparison: pet insurance (60–72%) vs. auto (55–75%)
            vs. health (80% ACA minimum).
          </p>
          <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3 text-xs text-gray-400">
            <span>Dog: $35–$60/mo avg</span>
            <span>·</span>
            <span>Cat: $20–$35/mo avg</span>
            <span>·</span>
            <span>NAPHIA 2024 data</span>
          </div>
        </a>

        <a href="/pet-insurance-vs-savings-account/" class="block rounded-xl border border-gray-200 bg-white p-5 hover:border-gray-300 transition-colors group">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2 group-hover:underline" style="color:#1B6B6D;">
            Pet Insurance vs. Savings Account: Which Costs Less Over 10 Years?
          </h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-3">
            $50/month in savings accumulates $7,350 over 10 years at 4% APY. $50/month in
            premiums returns $3,600–$4,300 in expected claims. Full side-by-side comparison
            of both strategies, including the catastrophic-event timing problem.
          </p>
          <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3 text-xs text-gray-400">
            <span>10-year comparison</span>
            <span>·</span>
            <span>Break-even analysis</span>
            <span>·</span>
            <span>Pre-existing condition tradeoffs</span>
          </div>
        </a>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Coverage & Exclusions -->
    <section class="mb-10">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-wide mb-4">Coverage &amp; Exclusions</h2>
      <div class="space-y-4">

        <a href="/what-does-pet-insurance-not-cover/" class="block rounded-xl border border-gray-200 bg-white p-5 hover:border-gray-300 transition-colors group">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2 group-hover:underline" style="color:#1B6B6D;">
            What Does Pet Insurance Not Cover?
          </h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-3">
            Complete exclusion reference: pre-existing conditions, routine care, breeding costs,
            waiting-period conditions, bilateral exclusions, and exam fees. Includes waiting period
            data across five major carriers and what "curable" pre-existing means in practice.
          </p>
          <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3 text-xs text-gray-400">
            <span>Exclusion table</span>
            <span>·</span>
            <span>Waiting periods by carrier</span>
            <span>·</span>
            <span>Pre-existing condition rules</span>
          </div>
        </a>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Decision Guides -->
    <section class="mb-10">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-wide mb-4">Decision Guides</h2>
      <div class="space-y-4">

        <a href="/is-pet-insurance-worth-it/" class="block rounded-xl border border-gray-200 bg-white p-5 hover:border-gray-300 transition-colors group">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2 group-hover:underline" style="color:#1B6B6D;">
            Is Pet Insurance Worth It? What the Data Actually Shows (2026)
          </h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-3">
            The industry pays back 72 cents per dollar. Whether that margin is worth it for your pet
            depends on breed risk, age, and what you can absorb. Covers when insurance consistently
            does and doesn't make financial sense — including for low-risk breeds and older pets.
          </p>
          <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3 text-xs text-gray-400">
            <span>72% loss ratio explained</span>
            <span>·</span>
            <span>When not worth it</span>
            <span>·</span>
            <span>Breed risk scenarios</span>
          </div>
        </a>

        <a href="/when-to-buy-pet-insurance/" class="block rounded-xl border border-gray-200 bg-white p-5 hover:border-gray-300 transition-colors group">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2 group-hover:underline" style="color:#1B6B6D;">
            When to Buy Pet Insurance: The Age, Breed, and Timing Math (2026)
          </h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-3">
            The pre-existing condition window is narrower than most people realize. Covers the age
            premium curve, breed-specific risk windows, what happens when you've missed the window,
            and the self-insuring alternative for older pets.
          </p>
          <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3 text-xs text-gray-400">
            <span>Age premium curve</span>
            <span>·</span>
            <span>Breed risk windows</span>
            <span>·</span>
            <span>Pre-existing condition trap</span>
          </div>
        </a>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Breed & Species -->
    <section class="mb-10">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-wide mb-4">Breed &amp; Species</h2>
      <div class="space-y-4">

        <a href="/expensive-breeds/" class="block rounded-xl border border-gray-200 bg-white p-5 hover:border-gray-300 transition-colors group">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2 group-hover:underline" style="color:#1B6B6D;">
            Most Expensive Dog Breeds to Insure — Top 15 by Lifetime Vet Costs (2026)
          </h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-3">
            Some breeds cost 3–4× more in lifetime vet bills than others. See which breeds generate
            the highest insurance claims, the specific conditions driving costs, and why premiums
            vary so dramatically across the breed spectrum.
          </p>
          <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3 text-xs text-gray-400">
            <span>English Bulldogs</span>
            <span>·</span>
            <span>Golden Retrievers</span>
            <span>·</span>
            <span>Great Danes</span>
            <span>·</span>
            <span>Dachshunds</span>
          </div>
        </a>

        <a href="/cats/" class="block rounded-xl border border-gray-200 bg-white p-5 hover:border-gray-300 transition-colors group">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2 group-hover:underline" style="color:#1B6B6D;">
            Is Pet Insurance Worth It for Cats? The Honest Math (2026)
          </h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-3">
            Cat insurance averages $387/yr vs. $749/yr for dogs — but so do expected claims.
            Covers urinary issues, kidney disease timing, indoor vs. outdoor risk, and the breeds
            (Maine Coons, Ragdolls) where the math looks different.
          </p>
          <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3 text-xs text-gray-400">
            <span>$387/yr avg premium</span>
            <span>·</span>
            <span>Urinary/kidney risk windows</span>
            <span>·</span>
            <span>Indoor vs outdoor comparison</span>
          </div>
        </a>

      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- CTA -->
    <div class="rounded-xl border-2 p-6 mb-8" style="border-color:#1B6B6D; background:#F0F7F7;">
      <h3 class="font-bold text-lg mb-2" style="color:#1B6B6D;">Calculate your specific situation</h3>
      <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        The articles above cover the general patterns. The calculator lets you input your
        pet's actual breed, age, and spending history — and see the math for your situation specifically.
      </p>
      <a
        href="/"
        class="inline-block font-semibold px-5 py-2.5 rounded-lg text-white text-sm transition-opacity hover:opacity-90"
        style="background:#1B6B6D;"
      >
        Open the calculator →
      </a>
    </div>

  </article>
</BaseLayout>
